  "text": "This study found that 53.7% of older people had suspected cognitive impairment or were at risk of dementia, with higher prevalences in women, in older age groups, and in those living at higher altitudes (a proxy for lower socio-economic and educational capital). This is well below the 75% reported among residents of Nepali care homes using the Cognitive Impairment Test 5, a difference plausibly explained by the greater age and multimorbidity of care-home residents. Conversely, a lowland study of 115 older adults found that 93.0% showed signs of mild cognitive impairment on the Montreal Cognitive Assessment 6. Both figures far exceed the 5% to 41% range reported in a systematic review of 80 studies 21 and the 22.2% found in a national study from China 22, underscoring how much prevalence varies with the instrument used and the setting. The high prevalence observed here may reflect limited education (almost two-thirds of Nepalis aged 60 and over are illiterate 23, 24) and social isolation arising from migration and changing family dynamics 12, 25. A high prevalence of cognitive impairment does not mean that most people will develop dementia, but it does indicate a raised risk; comprehensive assessment is therefore needed to rule out delirium and depression (pseudodementia), which is common in this age group 26\u201328.",
